 a total loss either  because we had Karen Gillan as nebula  and on paper this character really thin  but she did a lot in her performance  just finding motivations and with little  expressions in the way she delivered  lines she brought a lot of depth to that  character I'm actually really glad they  didn't kill her although that actually  brings me to my second point and that  would be the lack of death or more  accurately the lack of permanent death  for anybody aside from villains look  it's one thing for the heroes to live  that's actually par for the course in  any sort of Hollywood franchise it's  another thing to go oh they're dead nah  haha fooled you and then bring them back  Marbles pulled this trick on audiences  five times now they did it with Phil  Coulson Nick Fury Bucky Barnes Loki and  now brute look the thing of GRU  regrowing from a cutting of himself I  was prepared to give that a pass because  it does come out of the comic books but  it's  what pattern I just can't ignore any  isolated case of where they do this it's  fine unto itself but it's just it's the  repeat use of this you start adding  these all up and it starts a little  dangerously like Marvel comic books or  comic books in general because in comic  books nobody ever stays dead and if they  keep bringing that on board with the  movies then when they actually do decide  to kill somebody even if it's somebody  important it's gonna lose all its weight  because audiences are going to be going  oh yeah sure you killed him when's he  coming back come on comic book reduced  used to say nobody stays dead except for  Bucky Jason Todd and Uncle Ben you know  what they don't say that anymore why  because two out of three of those have  since been brought back to life Marvel  Studios has already pushes the point  that I can't take any death seriously  the only notable character death had  stuck so far has been Freya Thor's mom I  suppose if you want you can count  Yeltsin from the first iron man 